WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.763 --> 00:00:04.260
My name is Mo B. Dick.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:05.759
The B stands for Bodacious

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:06.767
I'm Maxxx Pleasure.

00:00:06.767 --> 00:00:07.713
Sigi Moonlight.

00:00:07.713 --> 00:00:08.676
Johnny Gentleman.

00:00:08.676 --> 00:00:09.749
King Molasses

00:00:09.749 --> 00:00:13.969
Also known as the sweetest and stickiest 
drag king you'll ever meet.

00:00:14.829 --> 00:00:19.851
When I first saw Drag Kings perform, 
I remember being like

00:00:19.851 --> 00:00:22.943
why hasn't nobody told me about this?

00:00:23.126 --> 00:00:27.401
I was completely mesmerized, wondering why
have I not done this before?

00:00:27.401 --> 00:00:28.740
I got hooked right away --

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.380
--just being on that stage and having 
the light hit me

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.500
and I feel like I'm finally me.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.535
I feel the fullness of myself 
when I'm in drag.

00:00:37.535 --> 00:00:40.360
As drag kings, we're stepping out of the box

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.251
of who we were told we were allowed to be.

00:00:43.251 --> 00:00:45.550
And people think it's only new now.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.070
But it's not new.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:50.472
I think the history of Drag Kings 
has been very much obscured.

00:00:50.472 --> 00:00:53.991
It hasn't always been the way 
that we understand it today,

00:00:53.991 --> 00:00:55.768
but it's always been there.

00:01:00.853 --> 00:01:04.360
Drag is just the performance of gender.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.960
The performance, the absence, 
the transformation of gender.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.640
The majority of drag kings are AFAB performers: 
Assigned Female At Birth.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:18.020
I do, however, know that there are AMAB performers
who also perform drag kings

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.000
and also lots of trans performers.

00:01:20.403 --> 00:01:25.220
It's about playing with masculine identities,
masculine performativity.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.539
Whether that's politicizing it, 
vulgarizing it

00:01:28.539 --> 00:01:31.501
making it a farce, making it silly

00:01:31.501 --> 00:01:34.103
You know, you can create this character,
you can say whatever you want 

00:01:34.103 --> 00:01:35.750
you can do whatever you want 
and people laugh.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:39.588
Whether it's clowning, striptease, 
dancing, singing.

00:01:39.588 --> 00:01:42.020
I might do a monologue to music.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:45.610
I draw a lot of inspiration from 
American rock stars.

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:50.380
I definitely am going for the bad boy with
a heart of gold type of thing.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.782
Can I say I'm a hoe on camera?

00:01:52.782 --> 00:01:53.914
Is that cool?

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:57.950
I'm kind of a hoe when I perform.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:02.043
That swag you get in the shower that like,
nobody sees, it feels like that.

00:02:02.043 --> 00:02:07.994
I am a Latin “papi,” which is like,
a Latin sex symbol kind of guy.

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:13.969
It surprised me, like I even started incorporating
like a lot of cultural elements into my drag.

00:02:13.969 --> 00:02:17.024
Even the palm hat that I'm wearing now

00:02:17.024 --> 00:02:22.650
a lot of men from where my mom's village
in Nigeria, Eniwari

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:27.880
a lot of them wear like big wide, 
very present hats

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:33.070
and they walk with big, broad shoulders
and drag takes me there.

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:37.280
Especially being a transmasculine, nonbinary,
Latinx person.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.280
I just want to make sure that every time I
hit the stage that

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:47.201
there's somebody in the crowd, hopefully,
that when they see me, they see themselves.

00:02:48.226 --> 00:02:53.634
Performing femininity was something that
I constantly felt like I was failing at.

00:02:53.634 --> 00:02:57.750
It's funny that transforming 
and becoming an alter ego

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.830
is what allowed me to finally accept

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.330
and love myself for who I was.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:06.340
And I started using they/them pronouns 
as Molasses

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.759
before I started using they/them pronouns
for myself.

00:03:09.759 --> 00:03:15.765
So it just has been this really rewarding
experience where...

00:03:15.765 --> 00:03:19.270
it's a way to navigate the world and make
sense of things.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:23.784
A lot of people don't know there's this 
long history of drag kings.

00:03:23.784 --> 00:03:29.200
A Chinese scholar who has male impersonation
dating back to the Tang Dynasty.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.250
And so male impersonation 
in the Chinese opera started then.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:35.860
Then it moved to the West.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:40.569
There were pioneers like Annie Hindle, 
Vesta Tilley, Ella Wesner.

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:45.940
To our knowledge, the first indigenous male
impersonator is Go-won-go Mohawk.

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:51.764
And she was in the early 1900s and she specifically
wrote a play

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:54.090
because she did not like the way

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.990
indigenous people were depicted in theater.

00:03:56.990 --> 00:04:00.951
During that time there were many 
females and lesbians

00:04:00.951 --> 00:04:04.450
that were doing male impersonation

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:11.138
as a way of expressing themselves and to protest
against what defines being a woman.

00:04:11.138 --> 00:04:14.438
And then moving on into the 1920s, 
Gladys  Bentley

00:04:14.438 --> 00:04:17.299
came out as the forerunner in Harlem

00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:22.380
dressing in men's attire,
was a prolific performer.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:25.487
Cross-dressing became illegal 
and it was a crime.

00:04:25.487 --> 00:04:29.870
People that came out and did that were real
true blue pioneers.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:33.155
Like Stormé DeLarverie.

00:04:33.155 --> 00:04:38.630
Someone who just epitomized 
what it meant to be Black

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:42.990
what it meant to be masculine, 
what it meant to be strong.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.624
What it meant to be swaggy.

00:04:44.624 --> 00:04:47.581
Male impersonators were more refined.

00:04:47.581 --> 00:04:52.420
It wasn't drag in the sense of the comedic,
camp element.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:56.796
The first time we see “drag king” in print
was in 1972.

00:04:56.796 --> 00:04:59.653
It's “The Queen's Vernacular: A Gay Lexicon”.

00:04:59.653 --> 00:05:02.500
And in that, it defines drag king,

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.127
So that was the first time that
we have found that in print.

00:05:06.127 --> 00:05:09.911
There was this huge, thriving scene in the
90s in New York City

00:05:09.911 --> 00:05:14.210
in San Francisco, in London, 
in other parts of the world.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:17.750
Drag, kings are growing and they're getting
bigger and bigger but...

00:05:18.116 --> 00:05:21.870
they still got a way to go when it comes
to representation

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:25.997
within the cabaret and the drag industry 
as a whole.

00:05:25.997 --> 00:05:29.727
RuPaul's Drag Race is so fantastic because
so many drag queens

00:05:29.727 --> 00:05:34.360
have become celebrities and pop culture figures

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:36.461
thanks to RuPaul's Drag Race.

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:39.071
That has not happened for drag kings yet.

00:05:39.071 --> 00:05:47.490
We don't wear heels, but we give story,
we give the drama, we give emotion

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:50.000
energy just like Queens do.

00:05:51.445 --> 00:05:55.000
Because we're not in the mainstream,
we don't have the same opportunities.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:59.185
As an AFAB person, I've had producers 
tell me before,

00:05:59.185 --> 00:06:02.325
that I was unsellable and that's why 
they didn't book me.

00:06:02.325 --> 00:06:08.141
What I do want is for folks to acknowledge
the power of misogyny.

00:06:08.141 --> 00:06:10.430
We are usurping male power and privilege.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:12.210
And that's still scary to people.

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:16.110
Our patriarchal society doesn't really take
kindly to the idea

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:20.594
that masculinity and manhood is something
that anyone can put on.

00:06:21.949 --> 00:06:23.344
It's tiresome.

00:06:23.344 --> 00:06:26.690
But the great news is that Drag Kings have
been in other

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:29.280
reality television competition shows.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:30.720
And won.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:34.022
We've got the Hugo Girl in 2018
who won in New Zealand.

00:06:34.022 --> 00:06:37.540
Landon Cider in 2019 who won Dragula.

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.370
Tenderoni in Chicago.

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:41.235
Who won in 2021.

00:06:41.235 --> 00:06:42.235
I am hopeful.

00:06:42.235 --> 00:06:44.384
I'm optimistic.

00:06:44.384 --> 00:06:46.755
And in the meantime, we're not waiting.

00:06:46.755 --> 00:06:52.080
This new generation of kings that are coming
up in the scene in the last 5 to 10 years.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.854
It's shaking up the scenes in 
a lot of different ways.

00:06:55.854 --> 00:06:58.090
We're making our own platforms.

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:01.260
We're also taking up space in platforms that
have

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:04.527
traditionally been dominated by by cis men.

00:07:04.527 --> 00:07:11.250
All of us are starting a revolution to be
seen

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.903
to fight for respect, because
that's what we deserve.

00:07:13.903 --> 00:07:17.520
I firmly believe that us drag kings 
will make our mark.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.414
Like, we're kind of the coolest.

00:07:19.414 --> 00:07:22.400
We've kind of always been the coolest because

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:28.143
we're kind of counterculture within a counterculture
within a counterculture.

00:07:28.143 --> 00:07:29.455
Like how punk is that?

